integrated_rules:
For any future policy proposal about whether Election Day should be a national (federal) holiday, apply the following condition-to-conclusion mapping:

1) Choose “MAKE Election Day a national holiday” when the available evidence supports ALL of: 
- A national Election Day holiday would increase voter participation by reducing practical scheduling barriers tied to school and work demands; 
- A national holiday would strengthen civic engagement and underscore that democracy matters (i.e., make voting a celebration rather than a chore); 
- Domestic and international precedent plausibly support feasibility/acceptance (e.g., 71% of Democrats and 59% of Republicans support it, and 27 of 36 OECD countries hold elections on weekends or holidays).

2) Choose “DO NOT make Election Day a national holiday; PRIORITIZE targeted reforms” when the available evidence supports ALL of: 
- A national Election Day holiday is a less reliable way to remove the main, practical barriers to voting than targeted access reforms; 
- Changing state laws requiring employer time-off through state law and implementing automatic voter registration are identified as more reliable approaches; 
- A national holiday would disadvantage low-income workers because federal law does not mandate private employers give paid holidays, so retail and service workers would likely still work.

3) If the evidence does not satisfy either full set of conditions (e.g., benefits are plausible but reliability/distributional impacts vs. employer time-off + automatic registration are unclear), then the argument does not determine the outcome (remain undecided under this decision rule).
